Open race with no incumbent, no published polls, and no expert ratings; fundamentals carry the estimate", "Unknown 2028 national environment, with 2026 midterm results likely to shape the map"], "rationale": "Washington has backed the Democrat in every presidential election since 1988, including a single-digit Harris margin in 2024 after Biden's near-twenty-point win, and no polls or ratings point to movement. With an open field and no state-specific evidence, the state's ten-point lean stands, with wide uncertainty.", "watch": "A strong Republican national showing in 2026 or a divisive Democratic nominating contest could narrow the margin, while a favorable environment could push the state toward Safe D.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Washington has been a reliable Democratic state in presidential politics, voting Democratic in every cycle since 1988. Harris carried it by single digits in 2024 after Biden's nearly twenty-point win in 2020, and Democrats hold the governorship, both Senate seats, and most of the congressional delegation. With the presidency term-limited and an open field on both sides, no candidate or campaign structure yet shapes the race, and no general-election polls in Washington have been published.\n\nThe forecast therefore rests on the state's underlying partisan lean, which favors Democrats by roughly ten points. An open race without an incumbent adds uncertainty, and the national climate in 2028 remains unknown, but nothing in recent coverage suggests Washington-specific movement toward Republicans. The state sits in Likely Democratic territory, with room for the margin to shift if the national environment changes sharply.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.873, "margin": 10.0}, {"d